\section{Peak and final size without solving}\label{sec:exact}
Dividing \eqref{eq:I} by \eqref{eq:S} removes time:
\begin{steps}
  \step $\dv{I}{S} = -1 + \dfrac{1}{\Rz S}$.
  \step Integrate from $S = 1$, $I = 0$:
        \begin{equation}\label{eq:orbit}
          I = 1 - S + \frac{\ln S}{\Rz}.
        \end{equation}
  \step The peak is where $\dv{I}{S} = 0$, that is $S = 1/\Rz$:
        \begin{equation}\label{eq:peak}
          I_{\max} = 1 - \frac{1 + \ln \Rz}{\Rz}.
        \end{equation}
  \step The epidemic ends when $I = 0$ again. The fraction left untouched, $S_\infty$, solves
        \begin{equation}\label{eq:final}
          \ln S_\infty = \Rz\,(S_\infty - 1).
        \end{equation}
\end{steps}

Figure~\ref{fig:phase} draws the orbit \eqref{eq:orbit} for the current slider value. The dashed line marks
$S = 1/\Rz$, the \emph{herd-immunity threshold}: once fewer than this fraction remain susceptible, each case
causes less than one new case and the outbreak shrinks.

\begin{figure}
\begin{tikzpicture}
\begin{axis}[xlabel={susceptible $S$}, ylabel={infected $I$}, xmin=0, xmax=1, ymin=0, ymax=0.7,
             width=10cm, height=6cm, grid=major]
  \addplot[orange, thick, domain=0.002:1, samples=400] {max(1 - x + ln(x)/\R, 0)};
  \addplot[gray, dashed] coordinates {(1/\R, 0) (1/\R, 0.7)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The path of the epidemic in the $(S, I)$ plane, equation~\eqref{eq:orbit}. Time runs from right to left.}\label{fig:phase}
\end{figure}

Table~\ref{tab:numbers} evaluates \eqref{eq:peak} and \eqref{eq:final} for a few reproduction numbers.

\begin{table}
\caption{What the reproduction number implies.}\label{tab:numbers}
\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|c|}
  \hline
  $\Rz$ & Peak infected $I_{\max}$ & Ever infected $1 - S_\infty$ & Herd-immunity threshold $1 - 1/\Rz$ \\
  \hline
  1.5 & 6.3\% & 58.3\% & 33.3\% \\
  2.5 & 23.3\% & 89.3\% & 60.0\% \\
  4.0 & 40.3\% & 98.0\% & 75.0\% \\
  \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\section{Vaccination}\label{sec:vaccine}
Vaccinating a fraction $p$ before the outbreak starts it at $S = 1 - p$. By theorem~\ref{thm:threshold} with
$S$ in place of 1, no outbreak grows if $\Rz(1 - p) < 1$, that is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:pc}
  p > p_c = 1 - \frac{1}{\Rz}.
\end{equation}
For $\Rz = \showparam{\R}$ this is the dashed line in figure~\ref{fig:phase}. The \href{briefing.md}{briefing}
discusses what this means in practice \cite{anderson1991}; \cite{hethcote2000} surveys the many extensions of the model.

The same mathematics of rates and thresholds appears in the \href{../oscillator/report.htex}{oscillator example}
(damping decides whether motion dies out) and in the \href{../heat/report.htex}{heat example} (each mode decays at its own rate).

